Falsifies `capability::a_stranger_reveals_nothing_about_its_liveness`.

The defect: `reap_decision` tests liveness before supervision, so a live thread
answers `StillAlive` whoever asks. Nothing is authorized that was not authorized
before (`Permitted` still requires the relationship), which is why this is the
plausible version of the defect rather than a straw one: it reads as a tidy early
return, every authorization test still passes, and what it costs is the *ordering*
the function's own doc comment calls deliberate. The channel it opens is a probe: a
supervisor invokes `REAP` on a tid it does not own and learns from the answer
whether some other supervisor's child is still running.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(reap_decision(supervising_rendezvous, invoked, true),
reap_decision(supervising_rendezvous, invoked, false))` under `kani::assume(supervising_rendezvous !=
Some(invoked))`. The second assertion in the harness,
`assert_eq!(reap_decision(supervising_rendezvous, invoked, true), Reap::NotSupervised)`, still
holds under this defect, because the `dead` path is untouched; it is the first
assertion, the one comparing the two liveness values, that catches it.

diff --git a/crates/capability/src/lib.rs b/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
index a5b8f24b3..eb5cd9e0c 100644
--- a/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
@@ -170,14 +170,11 @@ pub const fn reap_decision(
     invoked_rendezvous: u64,
     dead: bool,
 ) -> Reap {
+    if !dead {
+        return Reap::StillAlive;
+    }
     match supervising_rendezvous {
-        Some(rendezvous) if rendezvous == invoked_rendezvous => {
-            if dead {
-                Reap::Permitted
-            } else {
-                Reap::StillAlive
-            }
-        }
+        Some(rendezvous) if rendezvous == invoked_rendezvous => Reap::Permitted,
         _ => Reap::NotSupervised,
     }
 }
